### What is the First K Missing Positive Numbers problem?

Given an unsorted array with duplicates, negatives, and zeros, return the first k positive integers that do not appear in the array, in ascending order. This extends the single-missing-positive problem to finding multiple missing numbers. The target is O(n + k) time and O(k) extra space.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

import java.util.ArrayList;
import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.List;
import java.util.Set;

public class FirstKMissing {

    public static List<Integer> findFirstKMissing(int[] suffixes, int k) {
        int n = suffixes.length;

        // Phase 1: Cyclic placement
        for (int i = 0; i < n; i++) {
            while (suffixes[i] > 0 && suffixes[i] <= n
                   && suffixes[i] != suffixes[suffixes[i] - 1]) {
                int target = suffixes[i] - 1;
                int temp = suffixes[i];
                suffixes[i] = suffixes[target];
                suffixes[target] = temp;
            }
        }

        List<Integer> result = new ArrayList<>();
        Set<Integer> strays = new HashSet<>();

        // Phase 2: Scan indices for empty homes
        for (int i = 0; i < n && result.size() < k; i++) {
            if (suffixes[i] != i + 1) {
                result.add(i + 1);            // i+1 is missing
                strays.add(suffixes[i]);       // record the stray
            }
        }

        // Phase 3: Extend past n
        int candidate = n + 1;
        while (result.size() < k) {
            if (!strays.contains(candidate)) {
                result.add(candidate);
            }
            candidate++;
        }

        return result;
    }
}
```

### Why does cyclic placement work the same as the single-missing version?

The placement phase is identical to the smallest-missing-positive problem. Each value v in range 1 to n is sent to index v-1. Negatives, zeros, values greater than n, and duplicates already at their home are skipped. After placement, every in-range value that exists in the array sits at its correct home index.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

public class PlacementDemo {

    public static void trace() {
        int[] suffixes = {2, 3, 4};
        int n = 3;
        // After cyclic placement:
        // 2 -> index 1, 3 -> index 2, 4 -> out of range
        // Result: [4, 2, 3] (4 is a stray at index 0)
        // Index 0 holds 4 != 1, so 1 is missing
        // Stray: {4}
        System.out.println("After placement: value 1 is missing at index 0");
    }
}
```

### How does the index scan phase work?

Walk indices 0 to n-1. When `suffixes[i] != i + 1`, the value `i + 1` is missing from the array, so add it to the result. The value actually sitting in that slot is a stray: it is a number greater than n or a duplicate that got displaced. Record it in a set because it represents a number that exists in the array and must not be emitted later during the extension phase.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

public class ScanDemo {

    public static void trace() {
        int[] suffixes = {4, 2, 3}; // after placement of [2, 3, 4]
        int n = 3;
        // Index 0: suffixes[0]=4 != 1 → 1 is missing, stray={4}
        // Index 1: suffixes[1]=2 == 2 → correct
        // Index 2: suffixes[2]=3 == 3 → correct
        // Missing so far: [1]
        // If k=3, need 2 more from extension phase
    }
}
```

### How does the extension phase work?

If the index scan yields fewer than k missing numbers, continue counting upward from n+1. For each candidate, check whether it exists in the stray set. If it does, skip it because it is a number that was present in the original array. If it does not, add it to the result. Stop when the result has k entries.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

import java.util.HashSet;
import java.util.Set;

public class ExtensionDemo {

    public static void trace() {
        // After scan: missing = [1], strays = {4}
        int n = 3;
        int k = 3;
        Set<Integer> strays = new HashSet<>();
        strays.add(4);

        // candidate = 4: in strays, skip
        // candidate = 5: not in strays, emit → missing = [1, 5]
        // candidate = 6: not in strays, emit → missing = [1, 5, 6]
        // Done: result = [1, 5, 6]
        System.out.println("Result: [1, 5, 6]");
    }
}
```

### Why is the stray set bounded by O(k)?

The stray set only grows during the index scan, and only when a slot is wrong (meaning a missing number is found). Since we stop the scan as soon as the result reaches k entries, we add at most k strays. The extension phase only reads from the set, never adds to it. So the set size is at most k, keeping the extra space at O(k) rather than O(n).

```java
// Space analysis:
// - result list: at most k entries
// - stray set: at most k entries (one per missing found in scan)
// - Total extra space: O(k)
// - The array itself is modified in-place (O(1) extra for placement)
```

### What are edge cases?

Edge cases include an empty array (first k positives are 1, 2, ..., k), an array with a perfect 1-to-n sequence (missing are n+1, n+2, ..., n+k), and an array where all elements are the same value.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

import java.util.List;

public class EdgeCases {

    public static void test() {
        // Empty array, k=3: result = [1, 2, 3]
        System.out.println(FirstKMissing.findFirstKMissing(new int[]{}, 3));

        // Perfect: [1,2,3], k=2: result = [4, 5]
        System.out.println(FirstKMissing.findFirstKMissing(new int[]{1, 2, 3}, 2));

        // All same: [5,5,5], k=3: result = [1, 2, 3]
        System.out.println(FirstKMissing.findFirstKMissing(new int[]{5, 5, 5}, 3));
    }
}
```

```text
[1, 2, 3]
[4, 5]
[1, 2, 3]
```

### What is the time and space complexity?

| Phase | Time | Space |
|---|---|---|
| Cyclic placement | O(n) | O(1) in-place |
| Index scan | O(n) | O(k) for strays |
| Extension | O(k) amortized | O(k) for result |
| **Total** | **O(n + k)** | **O(k)** |

### What are common mistakes?

The most common mistake is forgetting to record strays during the index scan, which causes the extension phase to emit numbers that actually exist in the array. Another mistake is not stopping the scan early when the result already has k entries, which wastes time and inflates the stray set unnecessarily.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

public class CommonMistakes {

    // WRONG: not recording strays
    public static void wrongVersion(int[] suffixes, int k) {
        // After placement, scan for missing:
        // Index 0 has stray value 4, emit 1 as missing
        // Extension: candidate 4, NOT in strays (forgot to record!)
        // Emits 4 as missing, but 4 exists in the array!
        // Fix: record suffixes[i] in stray set when slot is wrong
    }

    // WRONG: not stopping scan early
    public static void wrongScanAll(int[] suffixes, int k) {
        // Scans all n indices even when result already has k entries
        // Wastes time and inflates stray set
        // Fix: add "result.size() < k" to scan loop condition
    }
}
```

### How is this used in a banking context?

At the National Bank of India, the onboarding team needs exactly k of the smallest unused positive account suffix numbers for a corporate batch opening. The suffix pool is an in-memory array of already-assigned suffixes mixed with duplicates and negative placeholders. Filing the valid suffixes home, listing the empty homes, and then extending past n while skipping observed strays produces the k free suffixes efficiently.

```java
package com.bank.onboarding;

import java.util.List;

public class AccountSuffixAllocator {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] assignedSuffixes = {3, -1, 4, 1, 3, 7};
        int k = 4;
        List<Integer> freeSuffixes = FirstKMissing.findFirstKMissing(assignedSuffixes, k);
        System.out.println("Next " + k + " free suffixes: " + freeSuffixes);
    }
}
```

```text
Next 4 free suffixes: [2, 5, 6, 8]
```
